description:
Create a Crawler project for a property — the same setup the dashboard's Crawler form performs, and the same thing the other MCP tools call a Site Audit (list_site_audit_projects, get_site_audit_summary and trigger_site_audit_crawl all operate on these projects). WRITE action — requires the write scope. Creating a project crawls nothing and spends no crawler credits; it stores the configuration only. Start the first crawl with trigger_site_audit_crawl and pass the returned audit_id: the trigger tool otherwise targets the most-recently-finished project, and a brand-new one has never finished a crawl. Crawler credits are separate from MCP credits and are spent then, one per crawled page. Defaults applied, none of them configurable on any surface: desktop device, the SeoCrawlerBot user agent, and an automatic weekly re-crawl once the first crawl has run. Returns property, audit_id, name, mode, urls_count and status. Errors: invalid_crawler_project_name (empty or over 255 characters), unsupported_crawl_mode, urls_required, urls_not_allowed, urls_outside_property (malformed URL or one on another host), too_many_urls, property_host_unknown. Pass the property ID (UUID) or URL (sc-domain:...) from list_properties.

Cost: 1 credit per call.

Examples:
```json
// Create a project that discovers its own URLs, then start it with trigger_site_audit_crawl using the returned audit_id.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","name":"Full site crawl"}

// Crawl a fixed list of the property's own pages instead of the whole site.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","name":"Key landing pages","mode":"list","urls":["https://example.com/pricing","https://example.com/features"]}
```
